The BOLT protocol — the irreplaceable artifact

This goes into docs/PROTOCOL.md first, before any other file. If the session ends early, this is
what makes the project rescuable.

GATT. API service 00010001-574f-4f20-5370-6865726f2121, characteristic …0002… (write + notify).
Anti-DoS characteristic 00020005-… must receive the 18 ASCII bytes usetheforce...band before the
BOLT accepts commands. Advertised name prefix SB-.

Connect sequence: scan → match SB-* → connect → discover → anti-DoS write → subscribe CCCD → wake.

Framing. 0x8D | flags | [TID] | [SID] | DID | CID | SEQ | payload | CHK | 0xD8, with escaping
(0x8D→AB 05, 0xD8→AB 50, 0xAB→AB 23) and CHK = 0xFF - (sum & 0xFF). Default flags 0x0A
(requests_response | resets_inactivity_timeout).

Two test vectors get asserted byte-for-byte in test/test-packet.js:
- A (captured, known-good) — wake: 8D 0A 13 0D 00 D5 D8
- B (derived) — matrix all-red: 8D 1A 12 1A 2F 01 FF 00 00 8A D8

This test runs with no hardware, no BLE, and no npm install — which is exactly why it's written first.

Key command IDs. DID 0x1A (IO; matrix commands need TID 0x12): matrix_pixel 0x2D,
matrix_one_color 0x2F, matrix_text_scrolling 0x3B, matrix_single_character 0x42,
set_all_leds_with_32_bit_mask 0x1A. DID 0x13 (power): wake 0x0D, sleep 0x01,
get_battery_voltage 0x03. Full tables with payload layouts go in PROTOCOL.md.

The throughput constraint that shapes the LED design. matrix_pixel is one BLE write per pixel — a
naive 64-pixel repaint costs 0.5–2 s. So led.js keeps an 8×8 framebuffer and flushes only changed
pixels, capped at 12 writes/tick; whole-panel effects use matrix_one_color (one packet); text uses
the firmware's own scrolling (one packet). Only "thinking" uses per-pixel writes, and only for a
≤8-pixel comet.

